WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:03.799
As you can tell from the grey speckled walls
behind me, I have found myself, in a stunning

00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:05.200
turn of events, in an airport.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.890
I've actually been stuck in the airport for
a couple hours, Hank, because my flight is

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.650
delayed, and also because I love airports!

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.620
But anyway Hank, I've been thinking about
The Art Assignment.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.360
One Art Assignment in particular, Jace Clayton's
The Quietest Place.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.730
For those of you who don't know, The Art Assignment's
a cool show produced by PBS about contemporary

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.240
arts, starring my wife, and to a much lesser
extent, myself.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.430
Jace's assignment was for us to take a walk
until we found the quietest place within walking

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:26.430
distance.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.070
Right now I can hear two separate TVs and
some children.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:31.470
And quiet is an interesting thing to talk
about on YouTube because attention has become

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:36.329
so fractured on the internet that there is
no longer room in YouTube videos for ANY silence.

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:39.771
Like Hank, I go back and I watch our videos
from 2007 with the jump cuts that are really,

00:00:39.771 --> 00:00:40.899
really slow.

00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:45.210
And this space between the jump cuts is just
absolutely unbearable.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:49.859
Like in those 3 silent frames, I think to
myself, "I might not survive until the end

00:00:49.859 --> 00:00:50.859
of this video."

00:00:50.859 --> 00:00:54.719
And in the many hundreds of responses to Jace
Clayton's Art Assignment I saw this again,

00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:55.719
and again.

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:58.729
People would be walking to their quietest
places, as I am now, and they would talk the

00:00:58.729 --> 00:00:59.729
entire time.

00:00:59.729 --> 00:01:01.749
It's only getting louder; this isn't working
at all.

00:01:01.749 --> 00:01:05.250
The other thing I noticed, Hank, is that people's
quietest places are incredibly loud.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.640
Usually it was loud with nature sounds, which
is fascinating, because it means on some level,

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.130
and I think this is true, we don't think of
nature sounds as being, like, sounds.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:14.920
We think of them as kind of a loud-quiet.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.360
No, this won't work, I can still hear the
TV.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.961
Anyway, it all made me think about on the
internet, and maybe even outside of it, quiet

00:01:20.961 --> 00:01:22.869
can feel kind of discomforting.

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:26.619
Just to get even through a few seconds of
quiet requires this intensity of focus that

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:28.149
we're not used to having to summon.

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:30.520
You know who knows the quietest place in an
airport?

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.030
TSA employees on a break.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:36.140
Okay, Hank, here we are, as far as I can tell,
the quietest place in the entire Indianapolis

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:41.289
International Airport, where by gate A4, the
carpet looks like this, and now Hank, together,

00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:46.590
we are going to enjoy and/or suffer through
a minute of silence together, alone with our

00:01:46.590 --> 00:02:47.370
thoughts.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.770
I can hear so much more than I could when
I sat down.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.540
Hank, happy VidCon, I'll see you on Friday.

